---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Pocketey is a tiny independent studio crafting polished 3D games made for portrait phone screens. Learn what we make, why we make it, and how the site is run."
  pageType="static"
>
  <article class="prose-page mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-amber-400 text-stroke-ink">
      About Pocketey
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg opacity-90">
      Pocketey is a one-person studio that makes free, polished 3D games for the
      browser. Every game on this site is hand-crafted to run on a phone held in
      portrait, load in under three seconds, and deliver a satisfying loop in
      under three minutes. No accounts, no installs, no trackers that follow you
      around the web.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">Why portrait, why three minutes?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      Most 3D browser games on the big aggregators are designed for landscape
      desktop play first, and only later squeezed onto a phone. We do the
      opposite. We start every prototype on a 9:16 canvas, with the assumption
      that the player is holding the phone with one thumb on the screen, while
      waiting for a coffee, a friend, or a bus. That single constraint changes
      almost every design decision: cameras get pulled in tighter, controls
      become a single tap or a swipe, scoring loops compress into bite-sized
      sessions, and every level has to teach itself in seconds. The result is a
      kind of game that, in our opinion, almost nobody else is making for the
      web today.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">What you will find here</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      Right now the catalog is small and curated on purpose. You will find tap
      puzzles where every move is a tiny chain reaction, endless runners where
      you backflip down a snowy slope, and short experiments that try a single
      idea well rather than a dozen ideas badly. The plan is to grow to roughly
      ten games over the first year, then thirty, then up to fifty if reception
      stays strong. Each game ships as a static bundle; we never stream content
      from a remote server during play, which means the game keeps working even
      when your connection drops mid-session.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">How the games are built</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      The technical backbone is Next.js with React Three Fiber for 3D rendering,
      and the portal that wraps everything is built on Astro for raw speed. All
      audio is generated on the fly using the Web Audio API rather than relying
      on licensed sample packs, which keeps the download size under a megabyte
      for most games. Visual style leans on bold cartoon colors, chunky
      outlines, post-processing bloom, and the Fredoka typeface — a deliberate
      counterweight to the muted minimalism of so many web puzzle games. The
      whole site is hosted on Cloudflare Pages and ships as static HTML, so it
      stays fast even from the other side of the planet.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">How the site makes money</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      Pocketey is supported by display advertising and game-network rewarded
      video. Ads are deliberately placed where they cannot interrupt the play
      itself: a small banner under the game frame, a single rectangle in the
      end-of-round modal, and an optional rewarded video for players who choose
      to watch one in exchange for a continue or a bonus. We do not run
      auto-playing video, full-page interstitials before play, or any ad
      placement that could be mistaken for the game UI. Honest, gentle ads are a
      conscious commitment; if a game stops feeling fun because of them, we
      change the placement, not the game.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">Who runs Pocketey</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      Pocketey is operated as a solo project. Game design, programming, art
      direction, sound, copy, and the website itself are all handled by one
      person, with help from open-source tools and open-license fonts. If you
      want to talk about a partnership, a feature request, a bug report, or
      simply want to say hello, the contact page has the latest channels open
      to the public. We try to read everything that arrives, even if a personal
      reply takes a while.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold">A note on language</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      The site and every game ship in English by default and add Japanese where
      practical, in line with our current main audiences. Other locales will
      be added as the catalog grows and as community demand makes itself
      heard. Thanks for stopping by — we hope at least one of these tiny worlds
      stays with you long after you close the tab.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
